1) Pod: 👉 Smallest unit in Kubernetes
           Runs one or more containers
           Application actually runs here
  Interview line:
                  Pod is the smallest deployable unit in K8s.
2) ReplicaSet: Maintains pod count
               Ensures required number of pods are always running
               If a pod is deleted → new pod is created.
  Interview line:
               ReplicaSet maintains the desired number of pod replicas.
3) Deployment ⭐ (Most Important): 👉 Manages application lifecycle
             Creates ReplicaSets
             Supports scaling, rolling updates, rollback
             Used in production
  Interview line:
               Deployment is used to deploy, update, and manage applications.
4) Service: 👉 Exposes pods to network
             Provides stable IP & DNS
             Pods IPs change, Service IP does not
  Interview line:
               Service provides stable access to pods.
5) Namespace: 👉 Logical separation
                Used to separate dev / test / prod
                Same cluster, different environments
  Interview line:
               Namespace logically isolates Kubernetes resources.
6) ConfigMap: 👉 Stores configuration data
                 Non-sensitive data
                 Keeps config outside application code
  Interview line:
               ConfigMap stores application configuration.
7) Secret: 👉 Stores sensitive data
              Passwords, tokens, keys
              More secure than ConfigMap.
  Interview line:
            Secret stores sensitive information securely.
8) Ingress: 👉 Manages external access
               HTTP/HTTPS routing
               Uses domain or path-based routing
  Interview line:
              Ingress manages external access to services.
9) Volume: 👉 Persistent storage
              Stores data beyond pod life
              Used for logs, uploads, DB data
  Interview line:
           Volumes provide persistent storage for pods.

